#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157040 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "61e9c047-0bbf-560d-b803-87703164db5b" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Vertical Parabola"}] :question/id #uuid "1abed37b-99f8-5298-bc7b-f80259c78398" :question/math-academy-id "e-7446" :question/problem "What set of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in(-\\infty, \\infty),$ generates the parabola shown below?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/assets/1a75a5242f3636efac8def7fc62458e1c1c6972ca79a1ab6fd73af47be622a7a.png)" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(-4,5),$ and passes through the point $(-2,3).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx - h = 2pt, \\qquad y - k = pt^2,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k) = (-4,5),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx + 4 = 2pt, \\qquad y - 5 = pt^2.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-2, 3),$ we can substitute $x=-2$ and $y=3$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2=2pt \\\\ -2=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =2pt \\\\ 1 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2 & =p{(\\frac{1}{p})}^{2} \\\\ -2 & =\\frac{1}{p} \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx + 4 = 2\\left(-\\dfrac12\\right)t, \\qquad y - 5 = \\left(-\\dfrac12\\right)t^2 ,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx + 4 = -t, \\qquad y - 5 = -\\dfrac12t^2 .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157041 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "337be080-59df-51bf-b0d5-14dba139b9c0" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Horizontal Parabola"}] :question/id #uuid "af2757f8-0392-54c8-b681-162ee13b1804" :question/math-academy-id "e-7447" :question/problem "What set of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in(-\\infty,\\infty),$ generates the parabola shown below?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/assets/8355891fbc605593a32dd100ee8c1c2e4324da0630755984158ec23f6d419ab9.png)" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola is horizontal, has its vertex at $(-3,2),$ and passes through the point $(1,0).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx - h = pt^2, \\qquad y - k = 2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k) = (-3,2),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx+3 = pt^2, \\qquad y-2 = 2pt.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(1,0),$ we can substitute $x=1$ and $y=0$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4=p{t}^{2} \\\\ -2=2pt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2 & =2pt \\\\ t & =-\\frac{1}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =p{(-\\frac{1}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 4 & =p\\cdot \\frac{1}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 4 & =\\frac{1}{p} \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx+3 = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)t^2, \\qquad y -2 = 2\\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)t ,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx+3 = \\dfrac{1}{4}t^2, \\qquad y -2= \\dfrac{1}{2}t .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157042 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "14079b03-66c0-5657-8909-a7d5ea35bfc0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of Parabola Given Its Parametric Equations"}] :question/id #uuid "780170af-a92e-5a87-b08d-d9d940d6906b" :question/math-academy-id "e-7448" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx-1 = -2t^2, \\qquad y+1 = -4t, \\qquad t\\in(-\\infty, \\infty).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =-\\frac{x-1}{2},\\,t=-\\frac{y+1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(-\\frac{y+1}{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation $t^2 = -\\dfrac{x-1}{2}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{16} & =-\\frac{x-1}{2} \\\\ (y+1)^{2} & =16\\cdot (-\\frac{x-1}{2}) \\\\ (y+1)^{2} & =-8(x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288353 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "61e9c047-0bbf-560d-b803-87703164db5b" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Vertical Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ad394608-abcb-5de2-b5ff-e06516eabe0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+1=t,\\,\\,y-2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288355} {:answer/id #uuid "1d2c872a-7d0c-506f-9378-3d42b3eee454" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-1=t,\\,\\,y-2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288356} {:answer/id #uuid "835e0a1a-b723-5064-8e33-8987a3a18d7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+1=t,\\,\\,y-2={t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288357} {:answer/id #uuid "6ecadcb3-f282-5d55-8bd9-2ec6296e661b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+1=2t,\\,\\,y-2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288358} {:answer/id #uuid "8e36d544-da4c-5b38-a3f0-cea6d0790f3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+1=\\frac{1}{2}t,\\,\\,y-2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288359}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad394608-abcb-5de2-b5ff-e06516eabe0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+1=t,\\,\\,y-2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288355} :answer-field/id #uuid "904337e3-8d48-5969-92a8-aaf05b0b62b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288354}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fedc73e9-6bd7-5662-845d-cc5946c6553c" :question/math-academy-id "q-106447" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/assets/4ac7f5cd3405e3839b829edc19c440c973521a0263f1aaa0a2f8245697779561.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(-1,2),$ and passes through the point $(1,4).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=2pt,\\,y-k=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(-1,2),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx+1=2pt,\\,y-2=p{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(1,4),$ we can substitute $x=1$ and $y=4$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2=2pt \\\\ 2=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =2pt \\\\ 1 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =p{(\\frac{1}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 2 & =p\\cdot \\frac{1}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 2 & =\\frac{1}{p} \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx+1=2(\\frac{1}{2})t,\\,y-2=(\\frac{1}{2}){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx+1=t,\\,y-2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288360 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "61e9c047-0bbf-560d-b803-87703164db5b" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Vertical Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f9a4fa1-d8c3-588f-98fe-b81a5881a397" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2=5t,\\,\\,y+3=\\frac{5}{2}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288362} {:answer/id #uuid "258895d0-7b61-57ed-9571-b5c3ac4f8ea7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2=-5t,\\,\\,y+3=-\\frac{5}{2}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288363} {:answer/id #uuid "d0b852c5-550f-595e-98d5-79ff3935f2ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2=-\\frac{5}{2}t,\\,\\,y+3=-\\frac{5}{2}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288364} {:answer/id #uuid "73d5206d-fdbf-580d-ab23-fa43572f6fd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2=5t,\\,\\,y+3=-2{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288365} {:answer/id #uuid "695f8cd3-739e-5183-bcb5-ddaf72274d5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2=-10t,\\,\\,y+3=-5{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288366}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "258895d0-7b61-57ed-9571-b5c3ac4f8ea7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2=-5t,\\,\\,y+3=-\\frac{5}{2}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288363} :answer-field/id #uuid "147672d7-0c0c-5a02-8cf9-6d414ff2f3b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288361}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3de3f5ae-3fc0-5c32-b996-56050a6bc315" :question/math-academy-id "q-106452" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/assets/0c37bb9e010bdb19de2afc8707b6cf1cef69638c9f4721c407c1593f3a387b87.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(-2,-3),$ and passes through the point $(-12,-13).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=2pt,\\,y-k=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(-2,-3),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx+2=2pt,\\,y+3=p{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-12,-13),$ we can substitute $x=-12$ and $y=-13$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-10=2pt \\\\ -10=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-10 & =2pt \\\\ -5 & =pt \\\\ t & =-\\frac{5}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-10 & =p{(-\\frac{5}{p})}^{2} \\\\ -10 & =p\\cdot \\frac{25}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ -10 & =\\frac{25}{p} \\\\ p & =-\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx+2=2(-\\frac{5}{2})t,\\,y+3=(-\\frac{5}{2}){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx+2=-5t,\\,y+3=-\\frac{5}{2}{t}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288367 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "61e9c047-0bbf-560d-b803-87703164db5b" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Vertical Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fa57c197-4d9c-5c03-96f0-8a7bead8fcd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-3=4t,\\,\\,y-3=2{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288369} {:answer/id #uuid "76a74879-e62a-5db9-bd67-4c17732534c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-3=4t,\\,\\,y-3=4{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288370} {:answer/id #uuid "b75efa1a-29cb-56b2-88d0-505084f1fd3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-3=2t,\\,\\,y-3={t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288371} {:answer/id #uuid "c2f086ac-b5c1-584f-9053-363ffda12355" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+3=2t,\\,\\,y+3={t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288372} {:answer/id #uuid "792a5ae4-4af3-59c8-84a1-7dc28471f787" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+3=4t,\\,\\,y+3=2{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288373}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa57c197-4d9c-5c03-96f0-8a7bead8fcd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-3=4t,\\,\\,y-3=2{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288369} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a1e4cfb-2b12-591a-a043-70d77182305d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288368}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cbfcd767-3d5f-5cb8-8eab-0b33a201040a" :question/math-academy-id "q-106455" :question/problem "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(3,3)$ and which passes through the point $(7,5)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=2pt,\\,y-k=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(3,3),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx-3=2pt,\\,y-3=p{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(7,5),$ we can substitute $x=7$ and $y=5$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4=2pt \\\\ 2=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =2pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{2}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =p{(\\frac{2}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 2 & =p\\cdot \\frac{4}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 1 & =\\frac{2}{p} \\\\ p & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-3=2(2)t,\\,y-3=(2){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-3=4t,\\,y-3=2{t}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288374 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "337be080-59df-51bf-b0d5-14dba139b9c0" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Horizontal Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8751437e-3810-5a6a-8fd5-be2296ef9a63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2=-2{t}^{2},\\,\\,y-3=-4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288376} {:answer/id #uuid "4b68f148-25b9-5e0e-903c-5cf5b6f424ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2=2{t}^{2},\\,\\,y-3=4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288377} {:answer/id #uuid "d30c50bf-dcb4-5c95-89a2-20f6e65a20d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2=4{t}^{2},\\,\\,y-3=2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288378} {:answer/id #uuid "80b24bdc-0bbd-58b8-bcdb-240784c71cb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-3=4t,\\,\\,y+2=2{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288379} {:answer/id #uuid "64a2e915-43f9-5524-8a15-aa424cb76d5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-2=2{t}^{2},\\,\\,y+3=4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288380}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b68f148-25b9-5e0e-903c-5cf5b6f424ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2=2{t}^{2},\\,\\,y-3=4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288377} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc168168-de42-55fb-977e-49bfcf0ca43e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288375}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8ad81bfb-009f-58b8-9a49-7041a80190ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-106459" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/assets/0e7558b9858668e619e3f7750efd76043981e2b833bcda7c7e462f6ecda1aa10.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola is horizontal, has its vertex at $(-2,3),$ and passes through the point $(6,11).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(-2,3),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx+2=p{t}^{2},\\,y-3=2pt.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(6,11),$ we can substitute $x=6$ and $y=11$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}8=p{t}^{2} \\\\ 8=2pt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8 & =2pt \\\\ 4 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{4}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8 & =p{(\\frac{4}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 8 & =p\\cdot \\frac{16}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 8 & =\\frac{16}{p} \\\\ p & =\\frac{16}{8} \\\\ p & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx+2=(2){t}^{2},\\,y-3=2(2)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx+2=2{t}^{2},\\,y-3=4t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288381 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "337be080-59df-51bf-b0d5-14dba139b9c0" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Horizontal Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2db5ca93-30e8-5da6-990b-d8942febd9ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+5=-3{t}^{2},\\,\\,y+3=-6t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288383} {:answer/id #uuid "ac2dd016-0425-5d40-ad73-06dd0e16f843" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+3=6t,\\,\\,y+5=3{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288384} {:answer/id #uuid "cdb78dab-6fb8-5638-8472-2e7c7e5f3c4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+5=3{t}^{2},\\,\\,y+3=6t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288385} {:answer/id #uuid "e6d413c9-bc84-50ff-ba3f-ca7e5422b364" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+3=3{t}^{2},\\,\\,y+5=6t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288386} {:answer/id #uuid "52281f8a-11f8-5a0b-942a-8477aca8faa8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+5=6{t}^{2},\\,\\,y+3=3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288387}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cdb78dab-6fb8-5638-8472-2e7c7e5f3c4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+5=3{t}^{2},\\,\\,y+3=6t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288385} :answer-field/id #uuid "77e16c48-9b1a-5f6d-9db8-0fd49008fb93" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288382}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "06a1eae4-f144-5f48-9020-f9d76a858d18" :question/math-academy-id "q-106460" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/assets/c6496d117c1b769474c353b0bfeef6836d21f080fb108752d55b8bb090dc0243.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola is horizontal, has its vertex at $(-5,-3),$ and passes through the point $(7,9).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(-5,-3),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx+5=p{t}^{2},\\,y+3=2pt.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(7,9),$ we can substitute $x=7$ and $y=9$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}12=p{t}^{2} \\\\ 12=2pt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 & =2pt \\\\ 6 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{6}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 & =p{(\\frac{6}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 12 & =p\\cdot \\frac{36}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 12 & =\\frac{36}{p} \\\\ p & =\\frac{36}{12} \\\\ p & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx+5=(3){t}^{2},\\,y+3=2(3)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx+5=3{t}^{2},\\,y+3=6t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288388 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "337be080-59df-51bf-b0d5-14dba139b9c0" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Horizontal Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e0eb2be-bc36-5cad-b102-f63bde897b30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-5=-6t,\\,\\,y+2=-3{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288390} {:answer/id #uuid "6cd55ad8-4124-5733-9f0a-59af107645f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-2=-{t}^{2},\\,\\,y+5=-2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288391} {:answer/id #uuid "83b5fd85-0f2d-57e9-a0c2-c598ef02e598" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2=-3{t}^{2},\\,\\,y-5=-6t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288392} {:answer/id #uuid "70dc7a8d-0093-57c2-bb9c-4a6b44a79748" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-2=-3{t}^{2},\\,\\,y+5=-6t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288393} {:answer/id #uuid "e0485994-9768-54ef-a94e-b9fc4f91eed2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-5=-3{t}^{2},\\,\\,y+2=-6t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288394}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "70dc7a8d-0093-57c2-bb9c-4a6b44a79748" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-2=-3{t}^{2},\\,\\,y+5=-6t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288393} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef91dae4-9dde-5751-9c5f-7995f46057bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288389}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "659918a3-ff24-5f01-abd6-269b3bb800ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-106463" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/assets/d7a5a3f962080fe457e5d35a581ee1340f1603f0c3bc64be4de6e7553f4402e1.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola is horizontal, has its vertex at $(2,-5),$ and passes through the point $(-5,-7).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(2,-5),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx-2=p{t}^{2},\\,y+5=2pt.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-10,7),$ we can substitute $x=-10$ and $y=7$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-12=p{t}^{2} \\\\ 12=2pt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 & =2pt \\\\ 6 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{6}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-12 & =p{(\\frac{6}{p})}^{2} \\\\ -12 & =p\\cdot \\frac{36}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ -12 & =\\frac{36}{p} \\\\ p & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-2=(-3){t}^{2},\\,y+5=2(-3)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-2=-3{t}^{2},\\,y+5=-6t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288395 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "337be080-59df-51bf-b0d5-14dba139b9c0" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Horizontal Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8823679c-4206-593e-9924-bba1b808b386" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-3=-4{t}^{2},\\,\\,y+1=-8t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288397} {:answer/id #uuid "9445efe6-ebcf-5fed-8a56-5ce164ddda33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-3=-\\frac{1}{2}t,\\,\\,y+1=-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288398} {:answer/id #uuid "b8ce72c8-fabe-53b4-9353-dea6391c44b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+3=-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2},\\,\\,y+1=-\\frac{1}{2}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288399} {:answer/id #uuid "9f933ccb-75fa-51e5-b46c-a9bcbb9b8b57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-3=-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2},\\,\\,y+1=-\\frac{1}{2}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288400} {:answer/id #uuid "32999de3-d390-5e7b-8e44-6029adc7ac8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-1=-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2},\\,\\,y+3=-\\frac{1}{2}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288401}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f933ccb-75fa-51e5-b46c-a9bcbb9b8b57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-3=-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2},\\,\\,y+1=-\\frac{1}{2}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288400} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9bd091d-91d9-5843-968d-693b832cddd7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288396}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "efd48b95-db5f-52c0-99e2-d4c8610ebe36" :question/math-academy-id "q-106465" :question/problem "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the horizontal parabola given by the Cartesian equation $(y+1)^{2}=-(x-3)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a horizontal parabola $(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h)$ that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nComparing the given Cartesian equation with the general case $(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h),$ we see that the parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(3,-1).$ So we have\n\n$$\nx-3=p{t}^{2},\\,y+1=2pt.\n$$\n\nNow, comparing the right-hand sides of the given Cartesian equation and the general case, then solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4p(x-h) & =-(x-3) \\\\ 4p(x-3) & =-(x-3) \\\\ 4p & =-1 \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-3=(-\\frac{1}{4}){t}^{2},\\,y+1=2(-\\frac{1}{4})t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-3=-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2},\\,y+1=-\\frac{1}{2}t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288402 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "14079b03-66c0-5657-8909-a7d5ea35bfc0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of Parabola Given Its Parametric Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c3974bc-a350-519e-8ce6-ba96c263be60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+2)^{2}=-4(y+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288404} {:answer/id #uuid "49983970-ecb2-5bca-bf85-b57a7c935a4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+2)^{2}=-(y+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288405} {:answer/id #uuid "5e96a228-acf3-54bb-8b82-104a11d94960" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+3)^{2}=-(y+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288406} {:answer/id #uuid "0c81681f-5e2a-5bf1-83b6-c8bc1d99c28e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+2)^{2}=(y+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288407} {:answer/id #uuid "44be291b-ea6e-568e-8944-73e67ac5cb78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+2)^{2}=-\\frac{1}{4}(y+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288408}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "49983970-ecb2-5bca-bf85-b57a7c935a4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+2)^{2}=-(y+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288405} :answer-field/id #uuid "772154c2-b716-5c23-8f34-9f1a094aff9e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288403}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "90e7bbda-fedf-5ef5-9757-a13f2517bec7" :question/math-academy-id "q-106503" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx+2=-\\frac{1}{2}t,\\,y+3=-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =-2(x+2),\\,{t}^{2}=-4(y+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(-2(x+2))}^{2} \\\\  & =4{(x+2)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=-4(y+3)$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(x+2)}^{2} & =-4(y+3) \\\\ (x+2)^{2} & =-(y+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288409 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "14079b03-66c0-5657-8909-a7d5ea35bfc0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of Parabola Given Its Parametric Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b7a9c3fe-b99a-57ac-b2b3-147b483ebeab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288411} {:answer/id #uuid "c1f56675-0ca9-546d-8c77-52d997de7ead" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288412}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b7a9c3fe-b99a-57ac-b2b3-147b483ebeab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288411} :answer-field/id #uuid "430e6a32-c5ac-536e-895d-3f04d1aa6890" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288410} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dbec2081-f15a-5ba0-aa49-e407ae6eddb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-y-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288414}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dbec2081-f15a-5ba0-aa49-e407ae6eddb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-y-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288414} :answer-field/id #uuid "1bb41120-1a14-5ab8-8ea6-dbbc84a01976" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288413}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4eca1d59-0a7a-514c-be56-d9295bc3b025" :question/math-academy-id "q-323153" :question/problem "Consider the following parametric equation of the parabola\n\n$$\nx-4=-\\frac{2}{5}\\,t,\\,y+1=-\\frac{4}{25}\\,{t}^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this parabola is\n\n$(x\\,-$\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =-\\frac{5}{2}(x-4),\\,{t}^{2}=-\\frac{25}{4}(y+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(-\\frac{5}{2}(x-4))}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{4}\\,(x-4)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=-\\frac{25}{4}(y+1)$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{25}{4}\\,(x-4)^{2} & =-\\frac{25}{4}(y+1) \\\\ {(x-4)}^{2} & =-(y+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288415 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "14079b03-66c0-5657-8909-a7d5ea35bfc0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of Parabola Given Its Parametric Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c18fbe89-3cbf-5047-a7ca-2f741f7315c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288417}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c18fbe89-3cbf-5047-a7ca-2f741f7315c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288417} :answer-field/id #uuid "584ae79f-f0ff-5cc5-8c9c-d7676af6e402" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288416} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7cebe2d5-9118-5944-8a19-b830c1b4b248" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288419}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7cebe2d5-9118-5944-8a19-b830c1b4b248" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288419} :answer-field/id #uuid "512da331-1f37-5611-b546-300d3b7ef138" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288418}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "63957822-af5c-5047-b47d-705f224584f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-323216" :question/problem "Consider the following parametric equation of the parabola \n\n$$\nx={t}^{2},\\,y-3=5t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this parabola is\n\n$(y\\,-$\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =x,\\,t=\\frac{y-3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(\\frac{y-3}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=x$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{25} & =x \\\\ (y-3)^{2} & =25x \\\\ {(y-3)}^{2} & =25x\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288420 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "14079b03-66c0-5657-8909-a7d5ea35bfc0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of Parabola Given Its Parametric Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52ea6cc9-037b-5caf-8807-ba3a131db9ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288422}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52ea6cc9-037b-5caf-8807-ba3a131db9ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288422} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f15f65c-b5da-5827-843f-3ab9389d9944" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288421} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "68c4bc08-c537-579b-bd5c-6f88aa96796f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4x+12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288424}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "68c4bc08-c537-579b-bd5c-6f88aa96796f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4x+12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288424} :answer-field/id #uuid "6304e4eb-2a97-5376-8811-3dcf21cea22e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288423}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9d85e680-26c7-5aac-9193-74809aef6804" :question/math-academy-id "q-323229" :question/problem "Consider the following parametric equation of the parabola \n\n$$\nx-3=-{t}^{2},\\,y+4=-2t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this parabola is\n\n$(y\\,+$\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =-(x-3),\\,t=-\\frac{y+4}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(-\\frac{y+4}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=-(x-3)$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{4} & =-(x-3) \\\\ (y+4)^{2} & =-4(x-3) \\\\ {(y+4)}^{2} & =-4(x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288425 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "61e9c047-0bbf-560d-b803-87703164db5b" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Vertical Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aaf02e36-db8b-556d-a1d9-932359ceef98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+4=-4t,\\,y+1=-2{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288427} {:answer/id #uuid "8a5b3f33-12a2-556c-b69c-8fc039817cc1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+4=-4t,\\,y+1=-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288428} {:answer/id #uuid "18666983-92bc-5d49-b058-32873deb7b00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+4=-t,\\,y+1=-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288429} {:answer/id #uuid "118182d5-98b7-5d39-b306-f6fa747972e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-4=-4t,\\,y-1=-2{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288430} {:answer/id #uuid "a43d2dac-12b0-586a-918c-59c5a0986bc1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-4=-t,\\,y-1=-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288431}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aaf02e36-db8b-556d-a1d9-932359ceef98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+4=-4t,\\,y+1=-2{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288427} :answer-field/id #uuid "da49ccce-4df5-5496-9bd2-9779aaf1a732" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288426}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d30c94fd-fa02-5be8-ad6f-2f4f9c02d497" :question/math-academy-id "q-84822" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/assets/6fcaa861fe507057f42c2194931bbc4a76d42891265ab40465832338cd8b5f4f.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(-4,-1),$ and passes through the point $(0,-3).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=2pt,\\,y-k=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(-4,-1),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx+4=2pt,\\,y+1=p{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(0,-3),$ we can substitute $x=0$ and $y=-3$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4=2pt \\\\ -2=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =2pt \\\\ 2 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{2}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2 & =p{(\\frac{2}{p})}^{2} \\\\ -2 & =p\\cdot \\frac{4}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ -2 & =\\frac{4}{p} \\\\ p & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx+4=2(-2)t,\\,y+1=(-2){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx+4=-4t,\\,y+1=-2{t}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288432 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "61e9c047-0bbf-560d-b803-87703164db5b" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Vertical Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d4742fad-09e4-509e-ad94-dee59336456e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+1=2t,\\,y-2=\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288434} {:answer/id #uuid "219adf0c-dfc0-5f77-a8d8-4fb1806d2dd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-1=4t,\\,y+2=2{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288435} {:answer/id #uuid "650a6ce7-9b00-530b-90ae-d297f7b59a93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+1=2t,\\,y-2=4{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288436} {:answer/id #uuid "4f7e0a0f-10e0-512f-a9ba-22366a5e9d97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+1=4t,\\,y-2=2{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288437} {:answer/id #uuid "c2a57fb0-cad7-5588-b8d3-d730ff66eb23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-1=2t,\\,y+2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288438}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4f7e0a0f-10e0-512f-a9ba-22366a5e9d97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+1=4t,\\,y-2=2{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288437} :answer-field/id #uuid "26302cf4-c209-56b7-bbed-7f797557ba1d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288433}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c739ca25-5d75-5711-8097-97fe57c475b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-84824" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/assets/bb83d1803b8b81992b8ed3f93019088beaba6db0fccf7ab707f03c55ed308678.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(-1,2),$ and passes through the point $(-5,4).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=2pt,\\,y-k=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(-1,2),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx+1=2pt,\\,y-2=p{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-5,4),$ we can substitute $x=-5$ and $y=4$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-4=2pt \\\\ 2=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4 & =2pt \\\\ -2 & =pt \\\\ t & =-\\frac{2}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =p{(-\\frac{2}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 2 & =p\\cdot \\frac{4}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 2 & =\\frac{4}{p} \\\\ p & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx+1=2(2)t,\\,y-2=(2){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx+1=4t,\\,y-2=2{t}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288439 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "337be080-59df-51bf-b0d5-14dba139b9c0" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Horizontal Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "481f85f8-bfe2-5199-a60f-cd2e3b0130d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+6=2{t}^{2},\\,y-2=4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288441} {:answer/id #uuid "1b8f13af-e4cd-5fbd-8eaa-86720f166fe2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-6=8{t}^{2},\\,y+2=4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288442} {:answer/id #uuid "60b04aee-002e-58a0-9ffe-21f7b9716dab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2={t}^{2},\\,y-6=2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288443} {:answer/id #uuid "0e59acd3-3242-55d3-a9b7-19247f4e24e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+6=4{t}^{2},\\,y-2=8t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288444} {:answer/id #uuid "39229461-a248-58d8-bd33-5f6f795d0367" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-2=4{t}^{2},\\,y+6=2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288445}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e59acd3-3242-55d3-a9b7-19247f4e24e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+6=4{t}^{2},\\,y-2=8t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288444} :answer-field/id #uuid "da975536-095e-5181-8cb0-81f6b31aa0bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288440}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a45072fa-409b-5a6f-a637-083f290f0cb1" :question/math-academy-id "q-84847" :question/problem "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the horizontal parabola given by the Cartesian equation $(y-2)^{2}=16(x+6)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a horizontal parabola $(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h)$ that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nComparing the given Cartesian equation with the general case $(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h),$ we see that the parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(-6,2).$ So we have\n\n$$\nx+6=p{t}^{2},\\,y-2=2pt.\n$$\n\nNow, comparing the right-hand sides of the given Cartesian equation and the general case, then solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4p(x-h) & =16(x+6) \\\\ 4p(x+6) & =16(x+6) \\\\ 4p & =16 \\\\ p & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx+6=(4){t}^{2},\\,y-2=2(4)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx+6=4{t}^{2},\\,y-2=8t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288446 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "14079b03-66c0-5657-8909-a7d5ea35bfc0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of Parabola Given Its Parametric Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32d0b6a3-4155-5e70-a0c7-78bc670f44d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-5)^{2}=8(y-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288448} {:answer/id #uuid "38917827-6af0-5c38-b487-5c5e1726bac6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-5)^{2}=4(y-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288449} {:answer/id #uuid "670b7ebf-f3db-5ec2-8cab-43a4597d1678" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+5)^{2}=-8(y+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288450} {:answer/id #uuid "bb6aaa2c-6bb8-5d0f-8dfb-ab79a6e5f6d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+5)^{2}=-2(y+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288451} {:answer/id #uuid "9e7d2afa-4da0-578d-99be-a65c3d8f3e08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+5)^{2}=-4(y+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288452}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e7d2afa-4da0-578d-99be-a65c3d8f3e08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+5)^{2}=-4(y+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288452} :answer-field/id #uuid "94c9ce65-48b0-577b-9094-c99c3fb2fe40" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288447}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0acf6f7c-0905-523f-bd2c-b81ada5c183d" :question/math-academy-id "q-84854" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx+5=-2t,\\,y+2=-{t}^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =-\\frac{x+5}{2},\\,{t}^{2}=-(y+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(-\\frac{x+5}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+5)^{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=-(y+2)$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+5)^{2}}{4} & =-(y+2) \\\\ (x+5)^{2} & =-4(y+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
